(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111600-the-entity-table-is-derivable-and-varies-so-it-is-the-layer-worth-adding")
  (:adr/topic "q9-parity-harness-table-layer")
  (:adr/title "The entity table is derivable and it varies, which is why it was the layer worth adding")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609111500-the-four-harness-layers-do-not-carry-equal-information"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Six pilots still had no parity harness, and finishing them would have been two
more batches of the same four layers. The previous ADR had just established why
that is weak: `page-limit`'s `8 of 8` is uniform across the cohort BY
CONSTRUCTION, because every pilot declares `default-limit 20` and `max-limit
100`. Repeating it six more times adds six rows and no information.

So this iteration added a layer instead of six repositories.

## Why the entity table and not one of the others

The layers the generator refuses -- store, filter, pagination, handler, expand
-- all need fixtures shaped to a repository's entities, and inventing those is
the failure the component generator had to be taught not to commit.

`oracle-table` needs no fixture. It hashes

    entity|plural|id-prefix|fields|required|coerce

for each spec, and the Clojure side builds the same string from the same
`entity-specs`. Both sides derive from the repository's own table. Nothing is
constructed for the test to consume.

It also has the property `page-limit` lacks: it varies. Two repositories cannot
agree on it unless their entity tables agree.

## It is a runtime check for a defect that passes amu check

The component generator carries an evidence floor because a component whose
tables still answer the TEMPLATE's prefixes compiles and returns `:ok true` --
measured, that is exactly what its first run produced. That floor is a
string scan over the generated file.

The table layer catches the same defect at RUN time, from the other side: if
`id-prefix-of` answers `abbrobot_par` where the spec says `accela_pro`, the
hashes disagree.

## Controls

A layer that always agrees is precisely what was being replaced, so two:

  **index sensitivity** -- compare the Clojure side's `table i` against the
  artifact's `table i+1`. Differs in 5 of 5. The comparison knows which entity
  it asked about.

  **per-repository signal** -- `com-adobe` and `com-agleader` share 0 of their
  6 table hashes.

## Verification

Both repositories: `amu check :ok true`, compiled to aarch64-macos, executed
through `tools/kexe_loader.c`.

    route-count   30 = 30
    as-bool       9 of 9
    page-limit    8 of 8
    table         6 of 6
    as-int        5 of 6, the \"12abc\" row differing

Coverage is now fifteen of twenty, and for the two repositories in this
iteration it is five layers rather than four.

## What is still not covered, and what that costs

The fixture-shaped layers remain absent everywhere. The table layer narrows the
gap in a specific way: it verifies that the component's DATA agrees with the
oracle's data. It says nothing about whether the component's BEHAVIOUR over that
data agrees -- that is what the store, filter, pagination and handler layers
would test, and they are still only present in the four hand-written harnesses.")))
